## Quick Setup

### Development

Clone, set up the workstation, and deploy {{ application_name }} onto the local stack:

```bash
git clone {{ software_repository }}
cd core
make onboard
make compose-deploy mode=reinstall apps={{ application_deploy_ids }} full_cycle=false
```

### Production

{% if application_is_host -%}
Install {{ application_name }} directly onto the target machine: clone the repository, install the OS prerequisites and the repository toolchain, then deploy against localhost over a local connection (no SSH, no container):

```bash
git clone {{ software_repository }}
cd core
bash scripts/install/package.sh
make install
source scripts/meta/env/load.sh

APP={{ application_deploy_ids }}
DOMAIN="<your-domain>"
{% if application_needs_clearnet_resolver -%}
DNS="<your-clearnet-resolver>"
{% endif -%}
{% if application_needs_service_provider_country -%}
COUNTRY="<your-country>"
{% endif -%}
TLS_MODE=self_signed
SSH_PUBLIC_KEY="<your-ssh-public-key>"
INVENTORY=inventories/production
infinito administration inventory provision "$INVENTORY" \
  --inventory-file "$INVENTORY/devices.yml" \
  --host localhost \
  --include "$APP" \
  --vars "{\"TLS_MODE\": \"$TLS_MODE\", \"DOMAIN_PRIMARY\": \"$DOMAIN\", {% if application_needs_clearnet_resolver %}\"networks\": {\"internet\": {\"dns\": \"$DNS\"}}, {% endif %}{% if application_needs_service_provider_country %}\"service_provider\": {\"company\": {\"address\": {\"country\": \"$COUNTRY\"}}}, {% endif %}\"users\": {\"administrator\": {\"authorized_keys\": [\"$SSH_PUBLIC_KEY\"]}}}"
infinito administration deploy dedicated "$INVENTORY/devices.yml" \
  --password-file "$INVENTORY/.password" \
  --diff -vv
```
{% else -%}
Run the published image to provision the inventory and deploy {{ application_name }} to a managed server over SSH. The image logs in as `root`, so the server must accept root over a public key, and the two key variables are the two halves of one key pair: the private half authenticates the deploy, and the public half is what the deploy keeps authorized for `root` and installs for the administrator account. Naming a public half that does not belong to the private one locks the running deploy out at its next reconnect.

```bash
APP={{ application_deploy_ids }}
HOST="<your-server>"
DOMAIN="<your-domain>"
{% if application_needs_clearnet_resolver -%}
DNS="<your-clearnet-resolver>"
{% endif -%}
{% if application_needs_service_provider_country -%}
COUNTRY="<your-country>"
{% endif -%}
TLS_MODE=self_signed
SSH_PUBLIC_KEY="<your-ssh-public-key>"
SSH_PRIVATE_KEY="<your-ssh-private-key-file>"
WORKDIR="$HOME/.infinito.nexus"

mkdir -p "$WORKDIR/inventories"
docker run --rm \
  -v "$WORKDIR/inventories:/etc/infinito.nexus/inventories" \
  -v "$SSH_PRIVATE_KEY:/run/secrets/deploy_key:ro" \
  -e APP="$APP" -e HOST="$HOST" -e DOMAIN="$DOMAIN" {% if application_needs_clearnet_resolver %}-e DNS="$DNS" {% endif %}{% if application_needs_service_provider_country %}-e COUNTRY="$COUNTRY" {% endif %}-e TLS_MODE="$TLS_MODE" -e SSH_PUBLIC_KEY="$SSH_PUBLIC_KEY" \
  {{ application_deploy_image }} bash -c '
    INVENTORY=/etc/infinito.nexus/inventories/production
    infinito administration inventory provision "$INVENTORY" \
      --inventory-file "$INVENTORY/devices.yml" \
      --host "$HOST" \
      --include "$APP" \
      --vars "{\"TLS_MODE\": \"$TLS_MODE\", \"DOMAIN_PRIMARY\": \"$DOMAIN\", \"SYS_SVC_SSHD_PERMIT_ROOT_LOGIN\": \"prohibit-password\", {% if application_needs_clearnet_resolver %}\"networks\": {\"internet\": {\"dns\": \"$DNS\"}}, {% endif %}{% if application_needs_service_provider_country %}\"service_provider\": {\"company\": {\"address\": {\"country\": \"$COUNTRY\"}}}, {% endif %}\"users\": {\"root\": {\"authorized_keys\": [\"$SSH_PUBLIC_KEY\"]}, \"administrator\": {\"authorized_keys\": [\"$SSH_PUBLIC_KEY\"]}}}" &&
    infinito administration deploy dedicated "$INVENTORY/devices.yml" \
      --password-file "$INVENTORY/.password" \
      -u root --private-key /run/secrets/deploy_key \
      --diff -vv'
```

The inventory under `$WORKDIR` is written by the container as `root`, including the vault password at `inventories/production/.password`. Run `sudo chown -R "$USER" "$WORKDIR"` afterwards to read it.
{% endif -%}
